higher protein content than fruits and vegetables, and municipal organic solid wastes have the highest lipid content. Several studies have determined the efficiency of BSF meal as a poultry feed [72], which generally shows that BSF meal can replace a significant portion of soybean meal in the poultry diet without affecting the product performance and quality. The studies on several fish species, including channel catfish (Ictalurus punctatus) [73], African catfish (Clarias gariepinus) [74], Nile tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus) [75], rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) [76], and Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) [77], successfully used BSF meal as a substitute for fish meal. These results indicate that BSF meal could be an alternative protein source for many fish species.
